<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/gender-and-society">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate lesson in Sociology. It assumes the foundational lesson Society
    and Socialisation — the family, school, peer and media agencies catalogued there are
    exactly the channels gender runs through, and this page calls gender the fastest track on
    which they operate. The method also carries over: take the taken-for-granted, trace it to
    structure, and count what changes.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>separate sex, gender and expression, and use cross-cultural variation as the evidence that scripts are social;</li>
    <li>trace gender socialisation through its agencies, and explain Bem's schemas and West and Zimmerman's "doing gender";</li>
    <li>explain Connell's hegemonic masculinity, and why both scripts bill their bearers;</li>
    <li>apply Ridgeway's three structural levels and spot an institution's default gender;</li>
    <li>quantify the care economy and the second shift, and read a pay-gap decomposition honestly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Gender sociology studies <strong>how societies script masculinity and femininity — and
    what the scripts cost and enable</strong>. Its method is defamiliarisation: notice the
    taken-for-granted (who does the dishes, who speaks first in meetings, whose job title
    sounds impressive) and trace it to structure rather than nature. The unit's three
    identity questions run through everything here: gender is the most heavily
    <em>taught</em> identity we have; its norms are the most continuously
    <em>enforced</em>; and its script is, measurably, <em>changing</em> — which makes
    gender the best available demonstration that identities are social products.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three layers, the structure, the care economy, the stall" widget="equationStep" config={genderCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each step is one section's thesis. Step 1 hands you the
    vocabulary and its warning — the scripts vary, so they are made. Step 2 is the
    architecture the middle sections build: identity, interaction, institution, reinforcing
    each other. Step 3 names the material core that most introductions skip — uncounted care
    work measured in shares of GDP. Step 4 states the punchline the final section tests:
    half a revolution, with the other half visible in every remaining gap.
  </p>

  <h2>Sex, gender, expression: the layers and what varies</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers are usefully separated. <strong>Sex</strong> is biological (chromosomes,
    gonads, anatomy — itself less binary than taught: intersex variations occur in
    roughly 1-2% of live births by broad definitions). <strong>Gender</strong> is the
    social assignment: the roles, expectations, statuses and identities attached to the
    categories. <strong>Expression</strong> is presentation. The founding evidence for
    treating gender as social is <strong>variation</strong> — across cultures and
    centuries. Margaret Mead's three New Guinea societies (1935) remain the classic: in
    one, both sexes were expected to be gentle and cooperative; in another, both fierce
    and combative; in a third, the Western script partly reversed. Dozens of societies
    institutionalise third or alternative gender categories (hijra in South Asia,
    muxe in Zapotec communities, two-spirit roles in many Native American nations) —
    recognised, named, sometimes ritual roles: if gender categories were biological
    constants, such institutional variety would be inexplicable. Within our own history:
    pink was a boys' colour into the 1920s; "homemaker" was a woman's destiny by law in
    some jurisdictions within living memory; women's university access is barely a
    century old in most countries. Variation is the natural experiment: whatever is
    biological in sex differences cannot explain why the <em>scripts</em> differ this
    much while being policed this hard.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the pink-for-girls, blue-for-boys convention is timeless, reflecting some innate colour preference. It is a fashion that inverted within living memory — early twentieth-century advice columns recommended pink for boys (as the stronger colour) and blue for girls — one of the cheapest demonstrations that a norm can feel eternal and be barely a century old.</p>

  <h2>How we learn gender: socialisation on the fastest track</h2>
  <p>
    Gender socialisation begins before birth ("it's a..." triggers different nursery
    colours, names, gift streams) and runs through every agency the unit has catalogued.
    The <strong>family</strong> assigns chores, tolerates roughness in boys and grades it
    in girls, talks more about feelings to daughters. <strong>School</strong> adds the
    hidden curriculum: reading schemes with gendered roles, subject-encouragement gaps
    that appear in maths and physics before age ten, teacher attention distributed
    unevenly. <strong>Peers</strong> enforce with brutal economy — the playground is
    where deviation gets its fastest sanctions. <strong>Media</strong> saturates: even
    as representation improves, role stereotyping persists in children's programming.
    Bem's <strong>gender schema theory</strong> gives the cognitive mechanism: children
    build schemas — "what boys do / what girls do" — and process the world through
    them, so socialisation becomes self-teaching: the child starts selecting, imitating
    and remembering gender-consistently. The interactionist capstone is West and
    Zimmerman's <strong>"doing gender"</strong> (1987): gender is not a role performed
    occasionally but a continuous production in every interaction — gait, voice,
    deference, competence displays — where others hold us <strong>accountable</strong>
    for performing our category correctly. That accountability is the control system.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the script is taught before birth and rehearsed in every
    interaction afterwards — self-teaching through schemas, policed through accountability.
    The next section prices that policing: what deviation costs, and what compliance costs,
    for both scripts.
  </p>

  <h2>How gender controls us: sanctions, hierarchy, and the cost of both scripts</h2>
  <p>
    Gender norms are enforced by <strong>informal sanctions</strong> — teasing, exclusion,
    the raised eyebrow — applied with striking speed to deviation. Iris Marion Young's
    "Throwing Like a Girl" traced how the feminine motor script (taking up less space,
    not reaching fully) is learned through a thousand small corrections; the masculine
    equivalent is "man up", "don't cry", the policing of tenderness. Formal control
    reinforces: dress codes, laws about who can drive, vote, own, inherit — most now
    formally equal in law (a real change this lesson must credit), with control
    migrating to the informal and institutional. Connell's <strong>hegemonic
    masculinity</strong> (1995) adds the field's most under-appreciated finding:
    masculinities form a <em>hierarchy among men</em> — the dominant script (tough,
    provider, dominant, heterosexual) subordinates other masculinities (caring,
    artistic, working-class variants, gay men) just as it dominates women. This
    explains data that a men-vs-women frame misses: why men die by suicide at 3-4 times
    the female rate in most rich countries, why boys' educational underachievement
    tracks the script ("schoolwork is unmanly"), why men report loneliness at the
    script's price of intimacy. The feminine script — <em>emphasised femininity</em>,
    in Connell's term — prices compliance, appearance and care. Both scripts are
    structures, and both bill their bearers.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> gender inequality is only about women being held
  back. The evidence above shows a two-sided constraint: the care burden, safety
  costs and authority gap fall mainly on women; emotional restriction, risk and
  violence, suicide and educational disengagement fall mainly on men. Both are
  products of the same script, which is why the field studies masculinities and
  why "what's in it for men" is a serious research and policy question.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> sanctions and hierarchy bill the bearers of both scripts — the
    care burden and safety costs fall mainly on women, emotional restriction and risk mainly
    on men. The next section scales the analysis up to the structures those bills are issued
    from.
  </p>

  <h2>Gender as structure: from interaction to institution</h2>
  <p>
    Ridgeway's synthesis (<em>Framed by Gender</em>, 2011) layers the analysis: gender
    operates at the <strong>individual</strong> level (identity and schemas), the
    <strong>interactional</strong> level (the doing-gender machinery, which also
    re-asserts difference in mixed-sex settings — studies of task groups show men
    disproportionately claiming airtime and credit even when equally informed), and
    the <strong>institutional</strong> level: workplaces designed around the
    two-carrier-family-with-a-housewife of the 1950s — full-time careers with no
    built-in caregiving — law, family law defaults, benefit systems. Institutions
    have a <strong>default gender</strong>: the unmarked worker is assumed
    child-free, mobile, backed by someone at home. That assumption is where the
    economy meets the care economy.
  </p>

  <h2>Language: gender's quiet enforcement channel</h2>
  <p>
    Language deserves its own section because it is the script's delivery system.
    English's <strong>generic masculine</strong> ("he", "mankind", "chairman") taught
    a male default for centuries — comprehension studies showing readers defaulting
    to male mental images prompted the shift to gender-neutral wording, singular
    <em>they</em> included (sanctioned by style authorities from 2019). The effects
    are measurable at decision points: Gaucher, Friesen and Kay (2011) found job
    adverts saturated with masculine-coded words ("dominant", "competitive") measurably
    reduced women's sense of belonging and their applications — words doing gatekeeping
    before any human gatekeeper acts. Across languages, the stakes scale:
    <strong>grammatical gender</strong> (French <em>le/la</em>, German
    <em>der/die/das</em>) forces gender categorisation onto every noun, and
    cross-national work (Prewitt-Freilino and colleagues, 2012) found
    gendered-language countries progressing more slowly toward gender equality than
    genderless ones — a correlation, not proof, but in the predicted direction.
    Institutions know the channel's power: France's Académie defended the
    masculine as "neutral" for centuries, and the state abolished the title
    "Mademoiselle" in 2012 — bureaucratic edits to a grammar of hierarchy. The
    mechanism matches the whole lesson: categories maintained in language are
    categories maintained everywhere else, one utterance at a time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> language keeps the categories maintained one utterance at a
    time — words doing gatekeeping before any human gatekeeper acts. The next section reaches
    the lesson's material core: who does the uncounted work everything else rests on, and
    what that assignment costs.
  </p>

  <h2>The care economy and the second shift</h2>
  <p>
    Unpaid care work — childcare, eldercare, housework, cooking — is the invisible
    infrastructure of every economy. ILO time-use data put women's share at roughly
    three-quarters of total unpaid care hours globally (about three times men's),
    and UN estimates value the work at 10-39% of GDP by country — larger in many cases
    than manufacturing. It is invisible in GDP because no transaction occurs, which
    means the formal economy runs on an uncounted subsidy. Hochschild's
    <strong>second shift</strong> (1989) documented its modern form: women entered
    paid work en masse (US women's labour-force participation roughly doubled
    1950-2000) while men's housework hours moved only slightly — leaving many women
    with a full paid shift plus roughly 15 extra hours of domestic work weekly, and a
    measurable <strong>leisure gap</strong>. Her "stalled revolution" names the
    mechanism of the persistent gaps: half the role revolution completed. The
    pay-gap evidence lands here. The raw US gap (women earning around 82-84 cents
    per male dollar) decomposes honestly: control for occupation, hours, experience
    and the residual shrinks to roughly 2-8% depending on method — but the biggest
    explained component is <em>itself</em> gendered: occupational segregation and
    reduced hours track the care assignment. The <strong>motherhood penalty</strong>
    (Budig & England: several percent of wages per child, with fathers often earning
    a <em>premium</em>) shows the mechanism up close. Field experiments catch the
    residue: in Goldin and Rouse's orchestra study, screens hiding candidates'
    sex increased women's probability of advancing substantially — and audits find
    identical CVs rated lower with women's names in male-typed fields. Goldin's
    synthesis of a century of data: the gap now concentrates where jobs
    <em>penalise</em> unpredictability and caregiving — "greedy work" — which is why
    flexible scheduling and parental leave for both parents are the current frontier
    policy levers, and why countries that normalised both (the Nordic pattern, with
    fathers' quotas in leave) show the smallest remaining gaps.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> unpaid care work is economically negligible because no one is paid for it. ILO time-use data put women doing roughly three-quarters of the world's unpaid care hours, and UN estimates value the work at 10-39 per cent of GDP by country — often larger than manufacturing. Its absence from GDP is a measurement convention, not a fact about the work's weight; the formal economy runs on the subsidy.</p>

  <h2>The evidence on change: what moved, what stalled</h2>
  <p>
    Change has been real and uneven — the syllabus asks for the evidence, so count it.
    <strong>Education reversed</strong>: women are now the majority of university
    students in most OECD countries (about 58% in the US) and outperform men in degree
    completion — a reversal within two generations. <strong>Employment</strong>: female
    labour-force participation converged towards male rates until the 1990s, plateauing
    after (the US stalled partly on absent care policy; countries that added childcare
    and leave kept rising — the natural experiment). <strong>Authority lags</strong>:
    leadership, professorships and boards remain male-majority everywhere, moving
    slowly (pipeline arguments only partly explain it — "glass cliff" research shows
    women gaining leadership disproportionately in crisis contexts).
    <strong>Attitudes liberalised fast</strong> — overt sexism declined sharply in
    survey data since the 1970s — yet <strong>behaviour lags attitude</strong>: the
    housework split moved by minutes a decade; occupational segregation by sex has
    been remarkably flat for decades (England's "uneven revolution"), including a
    debated "gender-equality paradox" where richer, freer countries show
    <em>stronger</em> sex-typing of field choice — evidence that choice is made
    against a cultural backdrop, not in a vacuum. The honest summary: formal
    equality is largely won in the rich world; the informal and institutional
    layers — who cares, who interrupts, who is assumed competent, who gets the
    flexible schedule — are where the remaining gap lives, and those are precisely
    the layers this page's concepts were built to see.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> because the raw pay gap shrinks once you control for occupation, hours and experience, discrimination is disproved. The decomposition is more honest than that: the largest "explained" components — occupational segregation and reduced hours — are themselves gendered outcomes tracking the care assignment, and audit experiments (identical CVs, screened orchestras) find the unexplained residue directly. Controls tell you where the gap hides, not whether it is innocent.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the three layers this lesson separates, and give one piece of evidence that the gender script varies across cultures.</li>
    <li>Job adverts saturated with masculine-coded words measurably reduced women's applications (Gaucher, Friesen and Kay, 2011). What does this show about language's role in the structure?</li>
    <li>Why does Connell call hegemonic masculinity a hierarchy <em>among men</em>, and what data does that frame explain that a men-versus-women frame misses?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Sex (biology), gender (the social role and identity) and
    expression (presentation) — separable layers that can vary independently. For variation:
    Mead's three New Guinea societies assigned gentleness or fierceness to both sexes, or the
    reverse of the West; closer to home, pink was a boys' colour into the 1920s. Whatever is
    biological cannot explain why the scripts differ this much while being policed this hard.
    (2) Language is the script's quiet enforcement channel: categories maintained in words
    gatekeep before any human gatekeeper acts. The finding shows structure operating at the
    interactional level — no individual discrimination needed for the words to shift who
    feels entitled to apply. (3) Because the dominant script subordinates other
    masculinities — caring, artistic, working-class variants, gay men — just as it dominates
    women. That frame explains the male suicide rate of 3-4 times the female rate, boys'
    "schoolwork is unmanly" underachievement and male loneliness — costs a men-versus-women
    reading has nowhere to put.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sex, gender and expression are separable layers; cross-cultural variation is the natural experiment proving scripts are social.</li>
    <li>Gender socialisation runs through family, school, peers and media; Bem's schemas make it self-teaching, and West and Zimmerman's accountability renews it in every interaction.</li>
    <li>Hegemonic masculinity is a hierarchy among men — both scripts bill their bearers: care burden and safety costs for women; emotional restriction, risk and suicide for men.</li>
    <li>Ridgeway's three levels — individual, interactional, institutional — reinforce each other; institutions have a default gender: child-free, mobile, backed by someone at home.</li>
    <li>Language is the enforcement channel: masculine-coded wording measurably deters applicants before any human decision.</li>
    <li>Unpaid care is valued at 10-39 per cent of GDP by country, roughly three-quarters of it women's; the second shift, the motherhood penalty and "greedy work" carry the modern pay gap.</li>
    <li>Change is real and uneven — education reversed, employment converged then stalled, behaviour lags attitude; the remaining gap lives in the informal and institutional layers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson Class &amp; Stratification picks up the material thread — pay gaps,
    occupational segregation and who can afford to specialise — and Deviance &amp; Crime
    shows the same accountability machinery sanctioning another deviation. For the workplace
    evidence, Goldin's "greedy work" is also the natural bridge into how labour markets
    price availability itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Biology's honest place.</strong> The field does not deny biology; it
    denies biology's sufficiency. Prenatal hormones shape average differences in
    some behaviours (interests, risk) with overlapping distributions; but
    institutions amplify or dampen those differences enormously (compare women's
    engineering enrolment across countries at similar income: it ranges from
    marginal to majority — same biology, different script). The current frontier
    is interaction: biology supplies predispositions, societies decide their
    price.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond the binary.</strong> Nonbinary and trans visibility stresses the
    three-layer model usefully: sex, gender identity and expression can vary
    independently, and institutions built on two-by-two assumptions (forms, sports,
    prisons, bathrooms) are discovering how much they encoded. Sociologically,
    the interesting datum is the same as Mead's: the more a society insists its
    categories are natural, the more effort it spends enforcing them — nature does
    not need policing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Will care work be redistributed, automated, or
    priced (care wages are rising as demographics age — the "care economy" may
    become this century's central labour question)? Does remote work loosen the
    greedy-work penalty or reintroduce the home as worksite with old scripts
    attached (early pandemic data suggested both, with women absorbing more
    childcare)? And can masculinities change at scale — or do falling male labour
    participation and rising male "deaths of despair" indicate a script crisis
    awaiting a new settlement? The evidence on change cuts both ways: scripts are
    demonstrably mutable, and demonstrably stubborn — which is exactly what a
    structure is.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mead, Sex and Temperament (1935); West & Zimmerman, "Doing Gender" (1987); Bem (1981); Connell, Masculinities (1995); Hochschild, The Second Shift (1989); Ridgeway, Framed by Gender (2011); Budig & England (2001); Goldin & Rouse (2000); Goldin, Career and Family (2021); England (2010); ILO and UN Women unpaid-care estimates; OECD education data. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself" widget="quiz" config={genderQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four questions walk the lesson's evidence spine —
    Mead's cross-cultural experiment, the interactionist "doing gender", the motherhood
